# cloud-init NoCloud seed — network configuration.
#
# Disables cloud-init's network configuration entirely.
#
# Without this file cloud-init has no datasource network config to use, so it
# generates a fallback for the primary NIC with BOTH dhcp4 and dhcp6 enabled
# and renders it to /etc/network/interfaces.d/50-cloud-init as:
#
#     iface <nic> inet dhcp
#     iface <nic> inet6 dhcp
#
# Debian 13 ships no DHCPv6 client (isc-dhcp-client was dropped from the
# release; dhcpcd-base backs ifupdown's IPv4 only). The inet6 stanza therefore
# fails with "No DHCPv6 client software found!" *after* the IPv4 lease is
# already bound — ifup exits 1, systemd fails ifup@<nic>.service, and dhcpcd is
# SIGTERMed, tearing down the address and routes that had just come up. The
# host boots with no IPv4 connectivity, on every boot.
#
# Network state belongs to Ansible (docs/automation-boundaries.md). The Debian
# installer's own /etc/network/interfaces stanza carries the host until the
# networkd role takes over.
#
# `network: {config: disabled}` is honored from the datasource, not only from
# /etc/cloud/cloud.cfg.d/: stages.py _find_networking_config() runs
# is_disabled_cfg() across every entry in network_config_sources, including
# NetworkConfigSource.DS.
# https://docs.cloud-init.io/en/latest/reference/network-config.html
network:
  config: disabled
